  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_1196472e673c48b4ad17696ef6b5d85c",
      "statement": "DSR corrects two distinct biases in the ordinary Sharpe ratio: multiple-testing inflation, where the maximum observed Sharpe over N candidates is upward-biased even when every candidate is pure noise, and non-normality, where negative skew and excess kurtosis both inflate the apparent significance of a positive Sharpe.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L51-L75",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:917ae93725d3d79e05c183f8ea5b4b3a0fdc14f24d4b8e1a68f47003e79bcfa0:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_2fa2914c7fa14f5f94e426fc749db7f7",
      "statement": "The DSR denominator's (gamma4 - 1)/4 coefficient is derived for the raw Pearson kurtosis convention where a normal distribution gives 3, and passing Fisher excess kurtosis instead would bias the denominator by a constant (3/4)*SR^2 and skew every DSR — the code calls scipy's kurtosis with fisher=False deliberately.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L76-L122",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:cc6815402a6fc5dbb30ed165e760da7f8cb6a54a97eaa27b889280a38af21397:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_889e9bb180884927baa90527cd859cf6",
      "statement": "DSR requires at least 4 bars to form skew and kurtosis and returns nothing below that, on a degenerate constant series, or on a non-positive denominator — but statistical power requires far more than that floor, with the spec's reference cases using 504, 1260, and 2520 bars.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L123-L134",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:1baf7897fd8601aa6ba4b5434c8b7256fc8ded736d80d5c12e9859dc77c71da4:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_6d916f2dd5584d39a329dcb14b92d963",
      "statement": "The effective-N correction converts a nominal trial count under an equicorrelation model so correlated parameter variants are not counted as independent tests, clamps negative diversifying correlations to zero as a conservative no-penalty-relief default, and evaluates the two-quantile expected-maximum approximation at max(2, N_eff) with a linear taper to zero across N_eff between 1 and 2 because the approximation diverges as N approaches 1.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L135-L154",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5683c3dfbe2237bd4352d58e121dc8d3eabdd1b923197b350f1b94f1e465fd2e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7a3dd843cae5495987939028c5a5e102",
      "statement": "PBO answers a different question from DSR: DSR corrects the winner's own Sharpe, while PBO asks whether the selection procedure itself is overfit, making it a property of the whole library rather than of one strategy.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L186-L194",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a9242e49fcdeeb008ba072168b7028ba034dc2b3a67f0b5c2b29bf9cd061bb12:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_55785df2dabe495ab99f8ad0e0b03300",
      "statement": "CSCV stacks aligned daily returns into a T-by-N matrix, partitions T rows into S equal time blocks, enumerates every way to choose S/2 blocks as in-sample, ranks strategies in-sample and looks up the winner's out-of-sample rank, and reports PBO as the fraction of splits in which the in-sample winner landed in the bottom half out-of-sample.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L195-L217",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2faa1761157056b3306be253147ffd5db06b7bcced802dd85d2311ac7375fec2:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ee9fb90073b2462297a48f536203bdeb",
      "statement": "The PBO implementation documents three limitations: library-level coupling means a strategy's verdict shifts when a neighbour is added or removed, small N makes the out-of-sample rank quantile coarse, and equal-length blocking drops up to S-1 trailing bars.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L218-L236",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:1d66f69fd34b3046a32c49c2efe5467d276bae3a2417ebeb79a81a1c4dcb5396:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_553b6a3984c247ca8a6a7976f25f7c43",
      "statement": "The walk-forward check is a single chronological hold-out rather than a rolling re-estimation, with no per-window refit and no purge or embargo gap at the train/test boundary, so a lookback indicator's state can straddle the split.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/quant/methodology.md#L239-L274",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:647bf7d65687c724455bd77a86d5f116c2cb1a6cc3e4e077f3ed814891840df5:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d9685d7304ad420287d3f9d0aba6f869",
      "statement": "CPCV is mathematically invalid on a single static one-dimensional return series because it would generate identical paths, so the function returns nothing unless a real two-dimensional combinatorial out-of-sample matrix is supplied and the gate reports the check as MISSING rather than silently passing.",
      "evidence": [
